Since about ULTRIX V2.2, maybe V2.0, ULTRIX HAS understood where EOT
is and dump(8), tar(1) and dd(1) have been modified accordingly.  The
problem is that dump(8) does these "nice" estimates of how much 9 track
tape a backup is going to require.  In order to get this algorithm to
give reasonable estimates you have to give it suitable values to plug
into the length and density.  Without reasonable values, you get
very poor estimates.  The estimates are kind of nice, but as I said
unnecessary on ULTRIX.

What is curious about the ULTRIX version of dump(8) is that if you
give it values, it trusts them.  If the actual tape length is shorter
than that estimated, it isn't a problem because the EOT handling code
kicks in.  If the actual tape is longer, it stops when it reaches the
estimated end, wasting the extra.  So you have a choice of good estimates
and potential tape wastage, or good tape usage.

If other vendors don't have EOT handling, that can probably be
held against them.  They have their problems, we have ours.  Nobody
seems to be perfect.

>
>In a similar vain, hooking up a SCSI disk drive to a Sun where you
>don't know the disk geometry (and even sometimes when you *do* know !)
>is an eternal pain in the rear end.  Are we really in 1993 ?

Around ULTRIX V4.0 we added an I/O control to get the geometry.  Newfs
and company use it, when one isn't available from /etc/disktab.  Whether
the I/O control can figure out the geometry is another issue...
