In <22vi9a$rfq@csugrad.cs.vt.edu> jaitken@csugrad.cs.vt.edu (Jeff Aitken) writes:

	>     I just wonder how DEC can stay competetive if they INSIST on
	>forcing their customers to stick with buying proprietary products.
	>Anybody with half a brain can obtain a SCSI expansion box (even I did
	>it) and get it to work just fine.

There are some additional issues that come into play besides just
finding the SCSI expansion box, but I do agree with you here.  My site
has over 100 SCSI disks running and shared amongs a VAXcluster, so I
am pretty familiar with some of the pitfalls.

A recent purchasing decision we had to make is a good illustration.
We were buying DEC DSP5200 drives (the OEM model of RZ73s) from a
third-party, and had been for many months.  Except for the drives
being loud during seeks (they sound sorta like rabid chipmonks), we
were quite pleased with them and had ordered over ten.  They ran
nicely and were a good value.

We were then faced with making a big order from DEC, and had to decide
whether we wanted to get eight more 2gig drives from DEC (i.e. RZ73s)
or DSP5200s from our third-party source.  Being adventurous, we went
with the RZ73s, which were a bit more costly, because our salesperson
said the BA350 cabinets they came in were much nicer than normal SCSI
cabinets (more plug-and-play).

The BA350 cabinets may be nice for sites using 7-disk arrays of 3.5"
SCSI drives, or they may be nice for large racks, but as standalone
cases they are pretty lousy for 5.25" RZ73s.

	* First, the SCSI connectors aren't standard: they are micro
	  connectors (like the ones found on DECstation 5000s).

	* Second, the SCSI connectors are on the backplane, requiring
	  an extra foot of length for each BA350 in a SCSI chain.

	* Third, the auto-configure SCSI IDs are nice for 3.5" drives
	  but lousy for 5.25" drives (you get SCSI 2 and SCSI 5).

	* Fourth, the BA350s take up a fair size footprint.

	* Minor problem: there is no power switch.

	* Minor problem: figuring out how to set the DIP switches for
	  non-auto-IDs and for splitting the drives into two busses is
	  not obvious.

	* And now DEC isn't selling carriers for these.

Are BA350 StorageWorks SCSI cases the wave of the future?  I don't
think so.  I did not enjoy paying more money for disk cases that have
less functionality than the defacto standards.  Maybe BA350s are nice
for rack systems with a lot of 3.5" drives (i.e. massive RAIDs), but
BA350s certainly aren't great for standalone 5.25" drives.  I will not
buy BA350s again.
