Hi there,

we use ultrix systemes (Ultrix4.3) and VAXen running VMS5.5 and UCX2.0
as TCP/IP software. On Ultrix I can use the "timedc" command to
check the time synchronization of almost every other UNIX system on
the network saying on the Ultrix4.3 system
    $ timedc clockdiff <unixhost>
    time on <unixhost> is 7 ms. ahead of time on <thishost>
but if I use a VAX running VMS/UCX I sometimes get (big) random numbers
and sometimes timedc claims that time is transmitted in nonstandard
format (the following commands were entered one after the other)
    $ timedc clockdiff <vmshost>
    time on <vmshost> is 17258891 ms. ahead of time on <thishost>
    $ timedc clockdiff <vmshost>
    time on <vmshost> is 10746676 ms. behind time on <thishost>
    $ timedc clockdiff <vmshost>
    time on <vmshost> is 9148463 ms. behind time on <thishost>
(VAXen running Ultrix are ok)
as far as I know, ICMP-Timestamp messages are not required, so 
UCX could be quiet if one does an ICMP-Timestamp-Request, but it
seems to me that they do an ICMP-Reply with a random number which
is wrong.

Does anybody know if this is a bug in UCX or if the behaviour is
legal?
-- 
