Hi, I log onto the VAX from a Personal Iris not an Indigo, but
it's probably the same, namely that the F9-F12 group of buttons
correspond to the top line of the keypad if you are coming in
from "winterm", but you will probably not get satisfactory
performance out of edt (lines on the screen will get messed up
and you'll have to keep hitting ^W to see what's really there).
Don't know about tpu.

If you create a wsh with /usr/sbin/xwsh -vt100 then the F9-F12
will be the top keypad line but now EDT should work ok.  (You may have to "set term/vt100" when you get onto the VAX depending on
whether you used DECNET or RLOGIN to get to the VAX.)

If you create an xterm on the Indigo with something like

/usr/bin/X11/xterm -132 -tn vt100

or

/usr/bin/X11/xterm +t

then the Gold key will be the "Num Lock" button, the "/" will
be the HELP button, etc, EXCEPT that hitting the "Num Lock" button
will turn on the Num Lock light and DISABLE the Gold Key. Hitting
the Num Lock button a second time turns off the NumLock light and
the next time you hit the Num Lock key it'll be a Gold Key again....
This sounds like a pain, but actually you just have to get into
double hitting the NumLock button to emulate one hit of the gold
key.

I usually use xterm rather than xwsh because (i) my hand is
used to the "EDT" keypad and (ii) xterm comes with Textronix
emulation (toggle against VT100 mode with Ctrl + Middle Button).

Good luck,

