In article <22mbiiINN6cc@topaz.bds.com>, ron@topaz.bds.com (Ron Natalie) writes:
>	LAT networks are easy because you just bridge everything together
>  with smart bridges and keep buying bandwidth and pray it doesn't all
>   down into slag on your watch. That's easy. Building ANY routed
>  network requires a knowledge of network design and routing. I don't know
>  anything about how DECNET does its routing but I assume it's not entirely
>  plug and play requiring no brain.
>
>DECNET != LAT.  You can route DECNET.  Nobody routes LAT to my knowledge.
>DECNET routing isn't really any more complex than IP routing.  Actually,
>it's slightly easier because there's better control on the standard (everything
>just tends to be a VMS machine running DEC's implementation).
>
>-Ron

DECnet is easier to manager than IP. It configures itself much more than
IP. For example, you can put any number of routers between two networks
and they decide among themselves which one takes the job. When it fails,
the next one takes its place. With IP, the gateway address has to be
manually configured into each end node ( the implementations I have seen )
and I am trying to figure out how I can automatically switch to another
router in case of failure. You have to know more to make IP work. It
is better if you know more to make DECnet work better but it can work
by itself pretty much. And the one that beats them all is AppleTalk, in
terms of easy configuration. 

Alain Martineau



