mjr@tis.com (Marcus J Ranum) writes:

>	DEC did the right thing with LAT, for the technology of the
>time when it was done. Nowadays it's simply not smart to write a
>whole new (proprietary, licensed, expensive, non-routing, application
>specific) protocol JUST to satisfy a small set of applications.

This is a bit too harsh.  In environments where routing isn't needed,
LAT is a big win.  There's no reason we can't have our cakes and eat
them too -- imagine a version of telnet which checks to see if the host
is on the local net, does LAT-like connects for local and routeable
connects for remote -- ie, the higher level application decides which
is the rational lower level protocol to use.  Admittedly this can be
pretty ugly, but so is sending an entire FDDI frame for a single
character.  IMHO, the Internet development folks have always placed a
high value on efficiency, and here we might have a case where the
efficiency overrides the loss of elegance and cleanness.  I know *I'd*
give up one ugly per application if it meant we could get rid of
one ugly per packet.
