In article <22lva5$c82@umigw.miami.edu>, byrne@rcf.rsmas.miami.edu (Charlie Byrne) writes:
> If I buy a Alpha PC, as opposed to the low end workstation (3000 300L),
> what am I missing out on?
> Is it possible/likely there will be future upgrades?
> I have the data sheets and raw numbers, I am looking for more general answers.

Avnet Computer just having mailed me a copy of the Alpha AXP Systems Handbook,
maybe we can answer that question without hassle. Of course you already
have these raw numbers, but they bear repeating.
           
                Clock   2nd level  Memory          I/O Bus
                Speed   cache      Min/Max/Speed 
DECpc AXP/150   150     512        16/128/115      SCSI-2 6@EISA
3000-300        150     256        32/256/50       SCSI-2 2@TURBOCHANNEL
3000-300L       100     256        32/256/50       SCSI-2

> What might I miss out on in the future?

The obvious thing you miss out on is that the EISA bus is actually a pretty
nice 32-bit bus. (EISA also handles older ISA bus cards too).

With 300 or 300L you would miss out on any neat device made for the PC
market that is EISA or ISA bus. Or maybe you would have to wait a long
time before the company could justify doing a turbobus version of the device.

Sure EISA is rated at 33MB/s max, and Turbochannel at 50MB/s. But if I
were concerned about speed I would be more concerned that the DECpc
has twice the 2nd level cache and more than twice the memory bandwith.

Rehashing the past, DEC should have come out with a VAXSTATION-3100 using
an ISA/EISA bus in 1989 and in a low-cost IBM-PC-CLONE style box
instead of the needlessly complex box they designed and built from scratch.

Maybe DIGITAL didn't want to cut into their QBUS market?

Presumably the 3000-300L is a good deal cheaper than the DECpc?

Interestingly enough, the PC market just just won't accept a cut-down busless
computer. Can anyone name a counterexample to that?

(besides some Laptops, the Mac SE, sort of).

-gjc


