In article <22mj22$a38@sol.TIS.COM>, mjr@tis.com (Marcus J. Ranum) writes:
> ron@topaz.bds.com (Ron Natalie) writes:
>>	LAT networks are easy because you just bridge everything together
>>  with smart bridges and keep buying bandwidth and pray it doesn't all
>>   down into slag on your watch. That's easy. Building ANY routed
>>  network requires a knowledge of network design and routing. I don't know
>>  anything about how DECNET does its routing but I assume it's not entirely
>>  plug and play requiring no brain.

It is plug and play, pretty much, unless you are building HUGE networks.  
Then you ought to worry about dividing things into areas. It still doesn't
require much brain. 

> Does DECNET use
> broadcasts for its routing information? If so, then it's got to be
> doubly pernicious (unless you buy lots of "smart" bridges) since on
> one hand you have to bridge it all together and on the other you have
> a source of plentiful broadcasts.

No, it uses multicasts for routing (on Ethernet anyway).  And they aren't that
"plentiful".  Any reasonably smart bridge will relay these only to segments
where DEC nodes have been heard from. 

> 	There's nothing wrong with DECNET. It's a perfectly reasonable
> protocol. Had DEC made the specs freely available and increased the
> addressing ranges, and not required royalties for implementing it,
> then it'd probably be the de facto standard now, not TCP/IP, instead
> of a legacy product that even DEC is transitioning away from.

As many others have pointed out, DEC has made the specs freely available, and
there were no royalties required.  (You STILL seem to be confusing DECnet and
LAT, at least in this regard.)  Up through Phase IV, that is. Not all of the
Phase V docs are available yet.  They apparently still *intend* to publish the
specs for Phase V, but are having problems getting them "out the door". 

DEC *did* make a mistake by not better publicizing the availability of the
specs (leading to lots of folks, like you, assuming that they were considered
proprietary).   Just getting the part numbers for the Phase IV docs used to be
like pulling hen's teeth.  But once you had the numbers, you could order them
with no trouble.  

> 	I used to work for a company that uses a lot of DECNET and
> LAT. I've directly seen the side-effects of bad network design when
> combined with pressure-to-bridge-everything to make LAT work. 

For any protocol, you can find a network design that won't work well. This
isn't specifically a DECnet or LAT problem. 

