reece@eco.twg.com (Reece R. Pollack) writes:
> |>Guess youve never used a TU58. A whopping 250 block storage
> |>capacity (or is that 250k?). God only knows what the transfer rate
> |>is. 
> As I recall, the controller for the TU58 was a DL-11E serial card.
> The transfer rate was 9600 BITS per second, and the modem lines were
> used to control the non-transfer functions.

(dig dig dig found it!)

It's jumper selectable from 150bps to 19200 bps.  Quite a few things ran
'em at 2400.

The TU58 talks to the controller using a packet oriented protocol; you
send a COMMAND packet to read a number of bytes from a position on
the tape, it seeks, reads and sends DATA packets back (128 bytes of
data per packet) until the request is fulfilled, at which point it
sends an END packet.  Writes are a COMMAND packet, and the TU58 sends
CONTINUE packetswhen it's ready for the next block. 

I don't believe it uses the handshake lines to talk to the drive, beyond
"are you alive" sorts of things.  

> Let's not forget that this wasn't classified as a tape drive, but
> as a DISK drive.

The operative term is "block structured device" rather than "disk drive".
After all, it did receive a "T" designation.

> |>Take about 10 minutes to boot a VAX 730 off this thing; loading most
> |>options like FP730, RB730...
> Let's not forget the 3-tape version of Standalone Backup. It took longer
> to boot S/A Backup from TU58 than it did to backup our R80 drive to a TS11.

It's five tapes under V5.x, if it's still supported.  I used to be able to
get a quiet afternoon by disappearing off to a satellite machine room
containing a mostly idle 11/750 to build S/A backup.  Because the serial
interfaces used for TU58s (DL11-E, DL11-W, VAX console port) are completely
stupid, have only single character silos and interrupt on BR4 (ie have to
fight with all other devices on the system for attention by the processor),
*any* I/O load on the machine would stuff up any attempt to do anything with
the TU58.  The TU58 protocol ("RSP", Radial Serial Protocol) alas lacks any
kind of retransmission mechanism, so any character loss would corrupt the
packet, you'd get a 133 bytes instead of a full 134 byte DATA packet (or
whatever) and the silly thing (after what seemed like days) would time out,
wouldn't recover and your hour or so of watching that idiotic red light
was wasted.

As for the TK50, I think the poor things are much maligned.  The things
have been out a long time, and when they were released were by far the
most cost-effective mass storage there was for DEC systems.  Remember that
the "competition" were the 9-track 1600bpi TS05 or the 6250bpi TU81; the
former could hold 36MB if you held your mouth right.  The TU81 cost a lot
more, for a 6250 bpi tape drive that could hold 150MB (again, if you held
your mouth right) and took up a lot more room.  The TK50 can hold 80-90MB,
for a third the price of the TS05 (and a fraction of the price of a TU81),
is faster, the drive is smaller and the cartridges are far easier to store.
It's no wonder they sold like hot cakes.  Even now, I can be fairly sure
that any DEC site I need to send a tape to has a TKsomething drive that can
read one of my cartridges.

Still, if you do your own maintenance, it pays to keep one or two spare
drives handy.....

