ben@WorldBank.org () writes:
> For first letters: R = disk (Rotating?), T = Tape drive, VT = Video Terminal 
> (I can't think of any other second letters here), L = Line (paper) device.
> There are some others, like PC, which are pretty obvious.

In general, the first letters are:
	A	Analogue interfaces
	B	Chassis ("Box"), cabling etc
	C	Card readers
	D	Digital communications interfaces.  Also backplanes
	F	Floating point units
	H	Cabinets
	K	CPUs & CPU related stuff (line clocks etc) ("Komputer")
	L	Line printers
	M	Memory
	R	Disk drives ("Rotating"? "Round"?)
	S	Storage arrays
	T	Magnetic tapes
	V	Video products (monitors, terminals, graphics)

The second letter in disk systems is usually specifies the interface, and
is usually one of:
	A	SDI (eg off an HSC, UDA50, KDA50)
	C	LESI interface (only RC25)
	D	ST506 interface (ie RQDXn controller)
	F	DSSI
	K	Various "own" interface devices (TK50, RK05 etc)
	L	RL controller 
	M	MASSBUS
	P	MASSBUS
	V	WORM
	X	Floppy drives
	Z	SCSI

For (modern) disks, the number specifies the form factor (first digit) and
some indication as to the capacity (second digit):
	14" 			Rx0n, Rx8n
	8"			Rx9n
	5.25" full height	Rx7n, Rx5n
	5.25" half height	Rx3n
	3.5"			Rx2n

Note that the capacities are consistent only within the same range, ie
an RZ23 is not bigger than an RA71, but an RA72 is.

Tapes mostly follow the same conventions as disks, but are a bit woolier
about the interfaces in older drives; TU covers a multitude of sins, from
a stupid serial interface (TU58), LESI (TU81), to MASSBUS (TU7x).

Ethernet stuff is always DEsomething, and usually mnemonic, eg DEQNA for
Digital Ethernet Qbus Network Adapter, DESPR for D.E. Single Port Repeater
etc.

"D" seems to be a category that things get thrown into if they don't fit
anywhere else.  Most are in the form Dx[i]nn-s, where x specifies the type
of interface, [i] is an optional bus type specifier, usually V or Q for
Qbus systems (a DxQnn is usually the Qbus equivalent of a UNIBUS Dx11, 
but the suffixes do *not* map), nn is '11' for things that will go in a
Qbus or Unibus backplane on a pdp11 (and usually a VAX); 32 means that it
only goes in a VAX.  The suffix (-s) is important, it specifies differences
in functionality between devices, where in other series it might only
specify a different power cord.  The second letters are:
	E	Ethernet (see above)
	F	Modems
	H,Z	Async multiplexor
	L	Stupid async port
	M,Q,U	Sync interfaces
	S,V	Sync/async interfaces
	T	DECtalk
	R	Parallel interface

Line printers are usually LA for asynch character printers, LP for parallel
printers, but tend to get confused when you get a "large" printer that
can be either or is graphics capable (LG, LXY etc).  LP11/LPV11 is the
designation for line printer interfaces.

"K" is usually in either Kx[t]ss, where ss is the PDP number (KA10, KD11
etc), or KAnnn for VAX processors (KA630 for uVAX II).  KDt11 processors
are Qbus systems; the t being the chipset (F = F11 (11/23, /24), J=J11
(11/73, /83, /93, /53, /84, /94)).  

Other K devices include line clocks, comms processors, coprocessors etc

CSS hacks are likely to be called any damn thing, but are usually mnemonic,
eg CS11-M (comms switch), IMP11-A (ARPANET IMP), PCL11-B (Parallel Comms
Link) etc.

Of course most of these rules have exceptions, and the M-number system of
identifying boards is a different ballpark altogether.

