In article <TWILLEY.93Jul24135824@dewey.nl.nuwc.navy.mil>, twilley@dewey.nl.nuwc.navy.mil (Jack Twilley) writes:
> What the heck is an 'airflow fault', and how is it sensed?

  The 86x0 has 2 airflow sensors by the intake filter, and 4 temperature sen-
sors (1 at the intake filter, and 3 at various places above the card cage. If
the inlet sensor reads > 33 degrees C or any of the outlet sensors read above
48 degrees C, or if the difference between any outlet sensor and the inlet sen-
sor exceeds 15 degrees C, the system will signal an alert and start a shutdown
timer. These are sensed by software. The middle sensor of the upper 3 will
also trip the input circuit breaker (via the EMM) if the temperature at the
sensor exceeds 60 degrees C.

  There's a 5th sensor on 50Hz systems (on the power transformer, which is only
fitted for 50Hz units).

  There is also a mechanical switch to make sure that the intake filter is in
place. If this switch trips, the system will trip the input circuit breaker.

  The airflow sensor itself appears to be an unencapsulated thermistor and a
driver board. It appears to measure the temperature drop caused by airflow,
by comparing a thermistor in the airflow path with a reference thhermistor in
the sensor, but without airflow across it. 

