>   >phase power. The blower has a 3-phase motor which won't spin without it. I
>   >don't know if the power supplies need 3-phase (I suspect they do), since with
>   >no fan, you will get an airflow fault and emergency power off before the sys-
>   >tem even starts to bring up the DC power.

>What the heck is an 'airflow fault', and how is it sensed?

Airflow fault means the air isn't flowing through the machine as it should.
On big machines this can be serious, since they can get *very* hot if the
cooling don't work correctly. By hot I mean hot enough to melt circuits.
I don't know exactly how the detector works, but I can think of several
constructions, such as checking if you have over/underpressure in the box,
or some kind of switch that pushed into the "correct" position by the
airpressure after the fan.

The real device is probably designed much smarter and simpler, but I
think you understand the general idea.
