In <CAp3F2.J5n@world.std.com> mbg@world.std.com (Megan) writes:

>ron@topaz.bds.com (Ron Natalie) writes:

>>> INdeed, DECtape was cool. The only tape, where if you were getting read errors,
>>> you just put your finger on the top of the tape, over the read head, to give it
>>> better contact...

>>The other neat feature is that it could read a block going in either direction.
>>The real DEC drivers (as opposed to the UNIX one) would write every third
>>block in one direction reverse and run in the other direction.  This is so
>>it could stream.

>If I remember correctly, this was how TOPS-10 (and I think RSTS) did
>it.  RT-11 accessed the drive using consecutive blocks.

OS/8 also access consecutive blocks. However, you can stream it by just
keeping the data flowing.

>As for reliability, I had heard that a DECtape could be three-hole punched
>down one side and continue to be readable due to the redundant information
>on the other side (other side lengthwise, not back vs. front)

That is true. Alla data was recorded on two tracks, left and right. So if
you punch a hole on either side, the data is still available on the
other track.

Another neat feature with DECtape, is that you can actually increase the
amount of data that can fit into a DECtape just by slowing the reel down
during the formatting, since the clock was recorded on yet another track
on the tape, and if the tape was slowed down, you could fit more blocks
into the tape. When reading or writing normally to the tape, the clock
is used to sync the whole thing. Data is actually 3 bits at a time, recorded
twice, and two clock tracks, making in 8 tracks.
3 bits was handy for many old DEC computers, but not the the pdp11, don't
know which scheme is used there.

