In article <22ks5e$sb1@Tut.MsState.Edu>, tbhudson@whale.st.usm.edu (Tramm Hudson) writes:
> 	Where can I find a complete list of DEC products numbers and
|> their real names?  I'm overwhelmed by LATs, TU80, RZ8, TK50s, etc and
|> need some help decoding all this cryptic stuff into something straight
|> and understandable like 132 colum printer, tape backup, xx meg HD, new 
|> tape backup, etc.
|> 	Any suggestions?
|

There is some method to the madness of all the numbers, though it is probably
somewhat removed from whatever the original concept was.  

The first letter generally indicates a generic group of device, such as a tape
drive (T).  The second character is the family that it belongs to.  So any 
device with TK is similar in concept to the TK50.  The number at the end is
usually some sort of rating for the product.  A lower number may actually be
a later product.  Also, the rating may not reflect what you expect.  An RA72
holds more data than an RA81. They seem to go back to unused numbers when
they get to 90.

The second letter may also indicate an interface.  For example, a Z seems to
indicate SCSI.  So an RA72 is generally for CI, but an RZ72 would be for SCSI.  
A TZ30 is a SCSI device as well.  This is certainly not always the case.  There
are SCSI TK50's, but that is determined by the extension (-xx), where xx is some
combination.

For first letters: R = disk (Rotating?), T = Tape drive, VT = Video Terminal 
(I can't think of any other second letters here), L = Line (paper) device.
There are some others, like PC, which are pretty obvious.

