>addressing ranges, and not required royalties for implementing it,
>then it'd probably be the de facto standard now, not TCP/IP, instead
>of a legacy product that even DEC is transitioning away from.

You seem to have a problem of separating DECnet from LAT.

DECnet is NOT proprietary and there has never been any requirement for
royalties. I'm told that DEC will sell you DECnet sources and charge royalties,
but you can roll your own with no problems. (At least, legal ones.)  LAT is
licensed and proprietary.

There are a long list of reasons TCP/IP is the defacto standard today. The main
one is that BSD 4.2 included a pretty good impelementation of IP for "free" and
there are LOTS of BSD based systems in the world.

The specs are and always have been available from DEC and at a fee no more
ridiculous than those charges for ITU, ANSI, or ISO specs.

