Ever meet a TS03?  I've never seen a TS11, but to the cpu they end up looking
the same, so I'll assume they're similar -- except that the '03 has a take-up
reel which is so placed, physically, that you _can't_ read or write a tape
longer than 600'.  Ever try copying a 2.9BSD distribution onto 600' tapes,
cleanly?  

And the interface for the damn thing... six or seven (I forget) cards in a
specially wired Unibus backplane.  Get even one wrong and you've got very good
odds of frying the whole thing, and having to buy another.  Luckily, Emulex
also made an interface for this.  Unluckily, it used the same backplane,
but had one _more_ card.  Don't get them confused!  Then, from this backplane
(in improbable places) you have Unibus extension cables (two, if I
remember right -- don't try this with a normal Unibus!) running to another
Unibus-looking backplane in a frame that's meant to sit under the drive,
but usually ends up on the floor getting kicked a lot.  There are some
more cards in this, and then, finally, a cable running out to the drive.


Oh, yes. One more thing.  It pays to leave the room while the drive's running.
You see, the cables all seem to be very vibration-sensitive...

Thank God one of Terry's friends fixed me up with a better 9-track by the
time I got my 750.  I can't imagine trying to use the TS03 to load up 4.3...

(or making the TS03 backplane run in a VAX, for that matter.  Anyone ever
try this?)

