>> Anyone know how to "turn off" the frame buffer on a VAXstation 2000,
>> so that it uses the serial port as its console?

> Assuming that you have already removed the monitor/keyboard/mouse, all
> you need to do is use a fully-wired cable to connect a teriminal to the
> printer port (9-pin connector) on the VS2000.  I'm not sure which wires
> are critical in this cable, but I know that only xmt/rcv/gnd are not
> enough because I tried that first.  I ended up using a pre-fabricated
> cable we got from DEC with a MicroVAX II, so I'm not sure what's in it.
> If you can't find it in the documentation anywhere, let me know and I
> will meter it for you.

I had the opportunity to check this out yesterday and found the info in
a VS2000 tech manual.  The normal printer cable is a BCC05 while a
"console" cable is a BCC08.  We have the BCC08's on all our uVAX-II's
(as they also have the 9 pin console out).  The manual says the cable
"jumpers pins 8 and 9 together" to indicate a console cable.

The connector pinout is pretty basic (signal names as in the manual):

   Pin     Signal         Pin   Signal      Pin   Signal
    1   Chassis ground     2   PTR_XDAT      3   PTR_RDATA
    4   No connection      5   +12 Vdc       6   No connection
    7   Chassis ground     8   Ground        9   FER_ENA

I haven't yet tested that anything beyond the PROM monitor knows to
talk to this alternate console.


