  voice-input household computer of the future.

   Mark, we seem to be dealing in extremes here.  Since computers are used
 in just about every device around these days, short of the toilet-paper
 holder (although the Japanese have a computer-controlled toilet complete
 with personalized settings), the average householder is not going to be
 inputting Maxwell's equations or QCD into his computer by voice.  I expect
 that he IS, however, going to be able to program his household computer,
 by voice, by saying something like this:

    "Computer, please wake me up at oh-six-hundred tomorrow, and please
  have the coffee ready then.  If there are any telephone calls between
  now and then, please forward these to John Smith at 555-3456."

    If the computer had a default method of wakeup already programmed,
  then it would not have to query as to how to do it.  The computer could
  recognize the voice-print of the person giving the command, and it would
  understand how to apply that person's defaults.  Perhaps another person
  in the same household would like to be awakened in another manner.  Also,
  the manner in which the coffee would be prepared would default to the 
  pre-programmed settings for each person in the household.

    Command understanding at about this level has already been put into an
  MIS (Management Information System) that was demoed for me.  I loved
  the way it handled defaults based on the context of what was already
  being displayed for the manager.  Of course, the input was typed in
  English words.  But I also saw on TV a demo of a program that listened
  to voice and typed WORDS.  Now, put the two technologies together and
  you have what I am suggesting above.  I believe it could be done now.
  And it will get more sophisticated as time goes on.  People will learn
  a new and more exact form of English as the computer mis-interprets
  ambiguities.  Perhaps there will arise a new English "dialect" (Lezz
  doesn't like that word, but I see nothing wrong with it) that we will
  use to talk to our house and appliances.  "The walls have ears".  :-)

