>Are you trying to solve a problem, or do you want to go to TCP/IP to join
>the bandwagon?  Because TCP/IP can't multiplex, it will most likely use
>more traffic than LAT.

	TCP/IP doesn't multiplex because it doesn't have to.
Terminal traffic-specific optimizations like keystroke multiplexing
belong at an application level not a protocol level.

        TCP/IP doesn't have a protocol specifically for terminal
traffic, like LAT. There are applications such as telnet and rlogin
that provide terminal access OVER TCP connections. Most versions
of these do not multiplex - some, however, do (such as HP's terminal
server work in the IETF). Adding multiplexing to your transport protocol
just for terminal traffic is stupid. Using a non-routing transport
protocol for terminal traffic is stupid. OOps - I just described LAT.
Currently stuff like telnet and rlogin are a drain on network
resources because of their bad habit of sending a packet per
keystroke. But that's a application level braindamage, not protocol
level braindamage. It's DEFINITELY braindamage, and it is being
fixed.

        LAT has destroyed more perfectly good networks than anything
else I can think of, simply because it doesn't route, and while it's
intended for LOCAL area transport, short-sighted network managers
tend to bridge their whole network into one gigantic mess, just so
they can use LAT. Hopefully they don't do this in hopes of realizing
some kind of performance gain. ;)

	Using IP is not "jumping on the bandwagon" it's a matter of
using a protocol that has become the de facto standard. DECNET and
LAT and OSI are history. If you look at the number (and cost) of
products becoming available based on IP, versus legacy protocols,
it's now simply a matter of good sense and ease of interoperation.
I predict that in the next few years you'll see less and less support
for proprietary protocols like DECNET (who wants to pay that licensing
fee? and the installed base is dwindling) which will further increase
the reluctance of ISVs to support them. VMS is suffering the same basic
decline.

	I find it ironic to see postings referring to jumping on
the IP bandwagon come floating across the Internet. ;) Or do you
think we're all running DECNET out here?  I think there are a few
OSI nodes out there, but they're mostly running dual stacks 'cuz
otherwise they have nothing to talk to either.

