We have 2 HP4M printers on our LocalTalk network.  The VMS queue
attached to these printers uses a Postscript symbiont.  Only
Postscript is sent to the printer.  They are only connected via the
LocalTalk interface card. We have Macs printing to them directly
through LocalTalk and also through the Mac Pathworks Spooler and this
works fine.  

The problem is with PCs printing through Pathworks.  Postscript files
generated by the PC HP4/4M driver have embedded Control-D (printer
reset) characters.  They also have an escape sequence of <ESC>%-12345X
at the very start and end of the document, this escape code is listed
in the HP4/HP4M manual as 'Universal Exit Language'.  These 2 type of
escape codes are not symbiont compatible.  The symbiont wants to have
control over resetting the printer and when it sees that the printer
has reset itself, it flushes the job.  Our HPIIID printer drivers
cause us no problems as there is no <ESC>%-12345X and the Control-D
characters are the very first and last characters so the spooler can
strip them off.
                                                                 
Here is a sample postscript document.

<ESC>%-12345X@PJL ENTER LANGUAGE =PostScript<CR><LF>^D%!PS-Adobe-3.0

                [middle part of document deleted]

fontHelvetica<CR><LF>% <CR><LF><CR><LF>%%EOF<CR><LF>^D<ESC>%-12345X


Manually editing the file and removing the Control-D (^D) characters
dosen't help nor does removing the <ESC>%-12345X sequences.  However
removing the string up to then Control-D will allow the file to print
with no problems.


In the PRINTERS.WRI file that comes with Windows 3.1 talks about
removing Control-D characters from your print jobs by editing the
WIN.INI file.  However the instructions didn't really match what was
in the WIN.INI and Microsoft Technical Support said they couldn't help
because HP hadn't given them the specs for the HP4/4M driver.  Anyway
just removing the Control-D characters won't be enough, we need the
string up to Control-D removed.

Does anyone have any hints/solutions for us?

Greg


