In article <22ks5e$sb1@Tut.MsState.Edu>, tbhudson@whale.st.usm.edu (Tramm Hudson) writes:

>	Where can I find a complete list of DEC products numbers and
>their real names?  I'm overwhelmed by LATs, TU80, RZ8, TK50s, etc and
>need some help decoding all this cryptic stuff into something straight
>and understandable like 132 colum printer, tape backup, xx meg HD, new 
>tape backup, etc.
>	Any suggestions?

A Systems and Options catalog would be a good place to start.  Check
with your nearest DEC sales person.  They can probably provide you
with a current one.  Older devices won't be there, but you'll get
a good clue from the current ones.  To take your particular examples:

LAT - Local Area Transport, a protocol used to communication between
      terminal servers and systems (and other terminal servers).

TU80 - A tape drive (the 'T' is the first clue).  As I recall it was
       UNIBUS based and support 800/1600 bpi 9 track tapes.  It was
       a streaming tape drive, but still pretty slow.  The low density
       did make it pretty easy to stream.  The transport made it 
       exceptionally easy to load.

RZ8 - Looks like an upper case version of an ULTRIX device name.  If
	it is an actual device the "RZ" suggests it being a SCSI
	disk drive.  We don't have any by that name though.  You'll
	tend to find:

	RZ2x - Home grown and buy-out 3.5" form factor drives.  If the
		name ends with an 'L' it's a not a full height drive.
	RZ5x - Buy-out 5.25" form factor drives.
	RZ7x - Home grown 5.25" form factor drives.

TK50 - The first Digital Linear Tape.  The capacity was 95 MB and would
	stream at an amazing speed of 45 KB/sec.  Probably the 2nd or
	3rd most hated tape drive we've ever built.  The basic transport
	was used with a wide variety of controllers; UNIBUS, Q-bus, VAXBI
	SCSI and the pseudo-SCSI of the VS2000.

	Later members of the DLT family are much improved, offering
	exceptional capacity and decent speed.
>
