TCP/IP migth not be such a grand idea. The internet people are supposed to run
out of addresse some time this year or next year. Currently it looks like the
OSI protocols take over at least the transport function (replaces IP).

To configure the equipement you need a good knowledge of TCP/IP, TCP/IP
routing, subnetmasks, etc. There are several books on the subject. The "Bible"
is Comers "internetworking with TCP/IP" volume 1.

Re discussion on LAT vs other protocols:
What one want is several transports working at the same time for a higher level
protocol. E.g. The higher end protocol tries to establish whether a non-routing
transport will suffice. In other  words a terminal server would first try to
use LAT, then CTERM (not sure if it is a transport), then XNS, then IP, then
OSI to reach a host.

As far as I know only PC LAN OSes can do this (Lan manager can be configured
to first try NETbeui, then TCP/IP). On Unix system V there is only a generic
transport interface. One can only configure RFS (or the high performance
networking option as I like to call it) to use one transport at a time (usually
either XNS or TCP/IP).



