mjr@tis.com (Marcus J Ranum) writes:
> 	The issue is interactive terminal traffic and bursty terminal
> traffic to terminal servers is very inefficient if you send each keystroke
> or small groups of characters in individual packets. So you multiplex
> the keystrokes or sessions or whatever term you want to use, and gather
> all traffic between two points up to the expiration of some timer or a
> packet's filling. None of this is rocket science.

It's worth noting that the original LAT terminal servers (on the DECSA
hardware) could support up to 32 ports; alternatively, you could "roll
your own" terminal server by bunging a bunch of DZ11s into an 11/34 or
similar and buying the LAT-11 software.  (The DECSA was actually an
11/24 based system, with protocol assist modules in the UNIBUS backplane
connecting to a custom backplane into which two-port line cards could
be inserted.  The same hardware, with single port sync line cards did
(and still does in some places) work as a DECnet router, SNA gateway or
X.25 DTE.)

The DECSA box was followed by the 8-port DECserver 100, which largely
lost the advantage of multiplexing, since the chances of more than one
terminal being active at any given moment are much smaller on an 8 port
box.  The DS100 was replaced by the DS200 (I often think of the DS200 as
a DS100 that works), and the DECSA terminal servers seemed to disappear
from the catalogues.  

The DS500, being a multi-interface box can get the advantage of
multiplexing however.


