>  by voice, by saying something like this:
> 
>     "Computer, please wake me up at oh-six-hundred tomorrow, and please
>   have the coffee ready then.  If there are any telephone calls between
>   now and then, please forward these to John Smith at 555-3456."

This is not natural language.  I am the only person I know who uses expressions
like oh-six-hundred.  (Yes, my wristwatch is set to display "military" time.)

[deletia]
>   And it will get more sophisticated as time goes on.  People will learn
>   a new and more exact form of English as the computer mis-interprets
>   ambiguities.  Perhaps there will arise a new English "dialect" (Lezz
>   doesn't like that word, but I see nothing wrong with it) that we will
>   use to talk to our house and appliances.  "The walls have ears".  :-)

Then let's get on with what you're *really* talking about:  we need to design a
new artificial language for voice control of things for which that makes sense,
and get people to learn it.  This is not really that much different from
teaching everybody BASIC in third grade, but it would allow the average user to
ignore complexities of the systems that are irrelevant to his use of those
systems.  The language may have English (and French, German, Swahili, etc.)
words in it, but it will be a language of machines, not men.  This is *very*
different from "natural language" command systems, and a *much* smaller problem
to solve.

I think that some of the strong reaction against STTNG-like systems is due to
that prickly sensation at the back of an analyst's neck, which says, "people
who know nothing about the problems that they propound are about to choose the
(inappropriate) solutions, and then ask you to implement them."  We've all been
stuck in situations where the product would have been much more robust,
cheaper, delivered more quickly, and just plain worked better, if only the
people who knew how to do that had been consulted before the spec.s were cast
in stone.  (Like some VP's decree that Unix will be used in all systems,
whether that makes sense or not.  See, I *am* working back toward the original
topic!)
