In article <1993Jul19.062707.2087@ugle.unit.no>, venaas@nvg.unit.no (Stig Venas) writes:
>Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec
>From: venaas@nvg.unit.no (Stig Venas)
>Subject: Fatal controller error on VS 3200, help needed
>
>I've got some problems with a VS 3200. Each time I boot, I get the
>following:
>
>Loading (a)vmunix ...
>
>Sizes:
>text = 733508
>Read error: bn = 736, Fatal Controller Error
>Read error: bn = 1152, Fatal Controller Error

[ And... ]

>ra0a: hard error sn 14195
>ra0a: Force Error Modifier Set: LBN 14195

There may be two different problems.  As noted by someone else the
controller has the replaced a bad block, but couldn't get a good
copy of the data and set the Forced Error flag to indicate this.
What you'll want to do is use icheck(8) with the -b option to track
down what that block belongs to and if it is a file, use ncheck to
track it down.  You can use radisk(8) to clear the forced error,
but if the block is an important program the program is now broken
and will likely cause something to break.

For the controller you may well have a broken RQDX3.  A broken
disk controller is a scary thing because it may be corrupting
data without telling anybody, claiming there are bad blocks where
they are actually good, etc.  If the system is covered by a field
service contract, get it checked.  If not, track down the person
responsible for hardware maintenance and let them know.  If that's
you, good luck.

