From article <22etu7$pe@calvin.NYU.EDU>, by liuyu@acf2.nyu.edu (liuyu):
> Hi,
>   Does anyone know what kind computer DEC VAX 11/780 is?

It was originally billed as a super-mini, although it quickly displaced
DEC's mainframe product line (the PDP-10, DECsystem 10, DECsystem 20
series of machines).

It was introduced in an era when the typical minicomputer occupied one 6
foot high by 19 inch wide EIA standard relay rack, including CPU and
perhaps a disk or tape drive.  I remember the The 11-780 being something
like 2 racks, each 6 feet high by 24 inches wide, just for CPU and
memory.  This had a PDP-11/23 with 8" diskette drive embedded in it for
running diagnostics, running the console terminal, and loading microcode
into the CPU.  You had to get additional cabinets to hold your disk
drives, and a typical system configuration occupied one wall of a room.

It required 240 volt power, if memory serves me correctly, and two large
window air-conditioners had a difficult time keeping up with the heat
when the outside temperature was over 70.

This was definitely not the kind of thing to put in your basement and
use for hacking, but it was a great departmental computer in its day!

				Doug Jones
