  Does anyone know what kind computer DEC VAX 11/780 is?  Is it a
  mainframe or a mini?  How big (physical size) and fast is it?
  Thanks for any info.

It (in my view) is a minicomputer (though some people tend to like to
promote things like that to the mainframe status, it doesn't meet it
in my estimation).  The CPU box is about five feet wide and is the
standard DEC lowboy hight of recent years (about 5' tall).  It's about
the normal 19" rack depth.  This holds the CPU, the power supplies for
the CPU and some memory and the console processor (an LSI-11) and it's
floppy disk.

You almost always have a second slightly wider than 19" cabinet (it has
19" mounting racks inside it) for a UNIBUS to which things like terminals
and in some cases disk controllers are connected to.  Another similar
cabinet houses optional memory, or additional backplane slots for higher
speed disk and tape controllers.

The index of speed is farely easy to measure.  It's the index that nearly
every compute benchmark is normalized to.  It's roughly equivelent to a
20 MHz 386, though it was the slickest in it's day and the first 32-bit
computer most of us got our hands on.

_Ron


