In article <CAF8qB.GzL@didymus.rmi.de>, andreas@didymus.rmi.de (Andreas Fassl) writes:
> 1 - Is it possible to use TCP/IP as transport instead of DECnet and if yes
>     how ?

  The DECserver 200 family uses neither TCP/IP nor DECnet. It uses LAT. DEC
doesn not offer any other protocols for the DS200. Newer servers, such as
the DS300, 700, and some 90's can support TCP/IP as well as LAT.

> 2 - Does anyone know something of the -internal- differences between
>     200-DL and 200-MC, especially if its possible to "upgrade"?

  There were two versions of the DS200/MC - one with a processor board and
a discrete connector board, and a later "reduced cost" version with only
the processor board (the connectors are on stilts on the processor board
in this flavor). The way to tell the difference is that the older version
has the 4 rear LED's in a vertical line, while the newer one has them in
a horizontal line. It *might* be possible to change the connector board
on an older one to upgrade a DS200/DL, but the card will likely cost you
more than the price of a used /MC. There may be other issues like firmware
as well. And of course, you'll have to take up sheet metal work to get the
connectors on the cabinet.

> 3 - When I do a "test port 1 loop external" with a speed of 9600, anything
>     is ok, at speed 19200 there is lot of garbage. Is this a problem
>     of the XON-flowcontrol or (PC via Telix connected as test port) of
>     the poor performance of PC serial I/O?

  Something's confused here. Saying "loop external" implies that there is a
loopback connector on the port, not a PC. On a DS200/MC you would get an
error saying that the loopback connector wasn't installed. You probably just
want the "TEST PORT 1" command. You shouldn't get any garbage. As you didn't
say if you were getting wrong characters or missing ones, it's hard to say
what the problem is. I'd try using the loopback connector (a little red
spoon-shaped device) in the "harmonica" with "TEST PORT 1 LOOP EXTERNAL" and
see if you get errors. If you do, it's the DS200 or the cable. If you don't,
it's either a PC problem or a baud rate mismatch (a common DS200 failure
mode is where the baud rate isn't what it claims. Thus, it can talk to it-
self but not anything else).

