In article <GSHAPIRO.93Jul7092612@monkeyboy.WPI.EDU>, gshapiro@monkeyboy.WPI.EDU (Gregory Neil Shapiro) writes:
> If you plan on upgrading any machines to DEC's new R4000/R4400 MIPS
> CPU, be aware that something in the root partition of older ULTRIX
> versions won't work with the new CPUs (we suppose it is probably the
> bootstrap).
> 
> The symtoms are that after installing the new board are trying to
> boot, the disk will spin up, read the boot blocks and then the station
> will reinitialize and try again.  No messages are output.
> 
> By trial and error we found that the root partition rebuild was the
> cure.  We were stung by this since we run a group of NFS-connected
> DECstations and have been copying new upgrades across the net rather
> than building our stations from the ground up when new releases come
> out.
> 
> Trying to think back about the R4000 upgrade that worked for us, we
> think that the root had been created under 4.3, but obviously 4.3A is
> safest.  If you are doing a full install of Ultrix 4.3A, this is done
> automatically, of course.  If not, this means a newfs on the 'a'
> partition of your boot drive.  This will erase everything in that
> partition and can't be done while the system is booted from that
> drive.
> 
> To accomplish this, we fully installed 4.3A on an external drive and
> used that drive on each of the machines we were upgrading.  Boot off
> genvmunix on each machine and backup the machine's boot drive 'a'
> partition to the external drive.  Then newfs the boot drive 'a'
> partition, restore its contents and reboot.  
> 
> Remember to be careful since you can screw up your boot drive.

Gregory and others,

Thanks for spotting this.  Engineering had the following comments,

	"It looks like WPI did their own homegrown style of upgrading 
	their systems to V4.3A.  As a result, many of their new R4000 
	systems ended up with a pre-V4.3A bootblock, resulting in 
	failures when they tried to boot their R4000 kernels.   

	"Based on the testing we did throughout V4.3A development, 
	doing a full install of V4.3A, or upgrading earlier version 
	systems to V4.3A will correctly install the new bootblock and 
	avoid the problems WPI ran into.

	"Knowing that some ULTRIX sites don't necessarily follow the 
	"supported" ways of installing/upgrading, we should have noted 
	in our Release Notes that this release includes a new bootblock.

	"The 4.3A release notes might have needed a note stating that 
	we fixed a >>bug<< in the bootblk code that was found during the 
	R4000 development [the bug has to due with the data being written 
	out inthe wrong ``address space'' for the MIPS chip.  On the R3000 
	that fact that is was wrong did not matter until the kernels got a 
	tad larger, in the case of the R4000, it does matter because of the 			cache  (a much larger kernel would have caused the R3000 to have 
	had same bug)].

The bottom line is that the standard ULTRIX installation, and upgrade process will work fine.  Older systems will have the local bootblk upgraded automatically (and silently) when the normal Digital defined proceedures are used.  However, we have notified the Customer Support Center (CSC) and they
are now aware of what is happening if others encounter the same situation.

Once again, thanks..

-- 
Russ Jones
Digital Equipment Corporation


