From: "Ray Harwood -- Data Basix" <rharwood@data.basix.com>

While I've not had much opportunity to consider benchmarks when purchasing
computer-based products, I tend to *not* use them when evaluating products from
different manufacturers.  This is probably because I tend to *not* trust equal
treatment of the conditions under which benchmarks are run.

I do, however, use the benchmarks of a selected manufacturer when deciding
between models.  I feel that a particular manufacturer has little to gain by
"loading the dice" on one model over another in the same product line.  I would
construct a cost-per-measuring-unit analysis to determine if I should recommend
going "up" the line or "down" the line to get more WhatMarks per dollar.
Especially on heavy-use systems like servers, where the actual processing
necessary may not be known in advance.  If the high end devices give more
bang-per-buck, we can potentially save an expansion cost later on, but if the
lower end model's ratios are "better", we can immediately save money and
perhaps buy a *second* unit later for expansion!

