From: Steve Vernon <srv@delta2.jhuapl.edu>

We rely on benchmarks to make purchasing decisions. Which ones you ask? Well,
our own. Here it is:
1. Accurately define our near term and future requirements. The users and system
admin people get together in a small group to do this.
2. Predict the future third party applications needed and identify which ones
are critical, nice to have, etc. Then determine which ones are ported to our
current and future environments.
3. Determine the "buy" as well as the upgrade and maint. costs associated.
4. Select 2 or three of the best suited machines based on the above as well as
performance specs, magazine reviews, etc.
5. Have the vendor "demo" the machines side by side with our exact applications
(and databases) running on them and observe the differences. We typically choose
items to run that present us the most problems in our current environment such
as large and complex databases, etc.
6. Pick the best one, buy it, make it work and get on with life until we feel 
the need to do this all over again.

Why do we do it this way?
1. Only with strong user input can the best choice be made. The users (many)
are the ones that will make or break a system in terms of productivity. The
users choose (collectively) the application software and hardware. System admin types can influence the choice, but cannot rule by decree!
2. We cannot forever be in search of the ultimate system, application software,
etc. We have real work to do!!!!!
3. Raw performance specs alone do not tell the entire story. Costs, training,
(and retraining) preserving our current investments, upgrade paths offered by
vendors for old equipment, etc. are all important factors.
4. Our management guides us in terms of money available. They also critique
our choices and decisions and make us aware of "the big picture" and the
future plans for our group. They trust us and we trust them. The also make us
responsible for our actions. We like this.!!!!
5. We have found in the past, that the costs associated with writing a spec and
the politics involved in formally benchmarking a system through a large group
of people will water down critical areas as well as cost too much money. The 
time spent by people is valuable also.
6. Keep the people involved small in number and keep the selection time frame
short. We don't want people to retire on this job nor do we want them to
become professional "benchmarkers" while the skills we hired them for degrade.

Ok, you have probably read enough. I can go into greater detail if you want.
The bottom line is that we are completely satisfied with our system choices
and we are constantly making them better and more productive. We attend trade
shows, conferences, read all the popular magazines and are not afraid to        investigate new things. We stay informed.
Thanks,
