From: jesse neri <neri@hobbes.nrl.navy.mil>

Yes, I use benchmarks to influence my purchasing decisions.  I have
found that the SPEC92 benchmarks (both int and fp) are good indicators
of the performance of a workstation across a mix of applications.  I
also ran the kernels of 4 of our science/engineering applicaitons on
boxes from four different vendors.  On average, the SPECfp ratings were
very indicative of the performance of the workstation, and are hence
quite useful.  For boxes with `structured CPUs' i.e. IBM and HP, there
are particular cases of my applications that ran extra fast, while
related applications, ran no faster.  For `neutral CPUs' i.e. SUN and
SGI, the performance was balanced for all applications.  Since we run a
mix of tasks, and are alway programming new ones, the balanced
performance on all applications seems more useful, and once again the
SPECfp ratings are very useful.

The other benchmarks like LINPACK are too specialized to be of much use
in predicitng the actual performance of the workstation on my mix of
jobs.  We have very serial codes that run faster on the workstation
than on a Cray Y-MP, and codes that vectorize well and than the Cray leaves
everything else in the dust.  It is not valid to take the results from
single task benchmarks and use them to predeict the performance of the
box on all tasks.  The SPEC stuff seems to average over many of these
performance oddities, and give a better predictor of performance.  Of
course, if you have a single application that the system is to live or
die on, than by all means test the performance of that particular app on
the particular box.

I have not been swayed by various graphics benchmarks, as 
1) I do not have a consistent feel for the relation between what the 
   benchmark tests and what I care about.
2) Our more immediate concern has been CPU performance anyway.
This may change as our visualization efforts increase.

