From: "System Admin (Mike Peterson" <system@alchemy.chem.utoronto.ca>
Organization: University of Toronto Chemistry Department


Yes - we do primarily "heavy duty" double precision number cruching,
and we look at Specmarks, Linpack and Livermore Loops, with Specmarks
being the most useful. The Specmark suite covers a good range of
applications, including a few that directly relate to theoretical
chemistry, which is the main use of our systems. Linpack can give an
idea what can be done with code that vectorizes, and how well a system,
even systems that don't have true vector hardware (but may have compiler
pre-processors to recognize some vectorizable constructs), can deal with
such codes. It is unlikely that a general user will get the Linpack
performance though, but we also run some of our own programs whenever
possible, and our overall performance numbers have usually followed
what the Linpack benchmarks showed.

The spread between integer and floating point performance can vary
greatly, and it seems that even within floating point codes, system
performance varies more with the specific application that it did in the
past, presumably due to system cache size and design interaction with
each program, and compiler optimization doing a better job on some codes
than others.

For general purpose crunchers, the standard benchmarks are useful, but
when buying a system that will be primarily used for solving specific
kind(s) of problems, you need to use those application(s) as the guide.

Mike Peterson, System Administrator, U/Toronto Department of Chemistry
E-mail: system@alchemy.chem.utoronto.ca


From: Art Stine <abstine@porsche.visix.com>


Rely on benchmarks? Yes and no... I wouldn't make a purchase decision soley based on the results of benchmarking. I think benchmarking is one of a group of considerations in making a purchasing decision, the others including: software support for the machine, service, price, warranty, reliability, life-span of machine/architecture,
vendors standing in the market, etc. Benchmarking serves to help indicate, in rough terms, where the performance of the machine is relative to competing machines running the same benchmark. The results of the benchmarks one uses (whether it be SPEC, MIPS, AIM, etc) is only useful if the benchmark reflects the type of work you expect to do with the machine. Not many of the published benchmarks reflect how well machines will do running alot of the various applications on the market today, other than some of 
the number-crunching apps. I think the best approach to getting useful results from benchmarking is to test the apps you are interested in running over time, and not putting alot of weight in things like SPECint/fp unless your work closely resembles the particular suite of programs used in the SPEC test.

