From: Dave Sill <de5@sws1.ctd.ornl.gov>


For company-wide procurement efforts, we've used both in-house benchmarks and
some of the more widely used standards.

We use in-house benchmarks when we can't find a standard benchmark that meets
our needs.  For example, to benchmark the portability of spaghetti Fortran, we
used a couple programs popular in the Oak Ridge scientific computing community.
To benchmark the performance of specific CAD/CAM applications, we developed
tests based on models used in current design work here.  We found standard
Fortran benchmarks were too well coded for portability to provide any
indication of the level of effort required to port them to different systems. 
Standard graphics benchmarks didn't take into account the varying levels of
performance that CAD/CAM applications exhibit, depending upon the quality of
the vendor's port or whether it did everything through the X Window System or
by accessing the graphics hardware more directly though GL or its equivalent.

Where we have successfully used standard benchmarks is in specifying CPU
performance.  For floating point intensive applications, we've used SPECfp92,
Livermore Fortran Kernels (aka Livermore Loops), and LINPACK.  For integer
applications we've used SPECint92 and SPECmarks.  We like the SPEC benchmarks
because they're real, large applications--not small synthetic benchmarks, so
they're not as susceptable to benchmark-specific optimizations.  SPEC's
reporting rules ensure that we'll be able to tell exactly what configuration
they were run on and what compilers, preprocessors, and optimizatoin options
were employed.

