From: Glen Reesor <glenr@cu51.crl.aecl.ca>


I work in an area where we do mathematical modelling of various components
of nuclear reactors, and thus floating point performance is the most
important factor for workstations.  The benchmarks on which I initially based
my evaluation were Linpack and SPECfp92.  Based on opinions expressed
on the net, and these two benchmarks, I narrowed the choice to two
machines (call them MachineA and MachineB).  MachineA was 10% faster on
the SPECfp92 and 24% faster on the 1000x1000 Linpack.

I later received numbers for the following (floating point intensive) 
benchmarks:
   DN&R Labs CPU2 benchmark (MachineA 52% faster)
   CERN Benchmark Suite     (MachineA 10% faster)
   SPECrate_fp92 Benchmark  (MachineA 32% faster)

However, upon receiving MachineA (actually the model lower, but we scaled
results according to clock speeds) and MachineB for our own testing,
we showed MachineB beating MachineA by at least 40%, and sometimes up to

There are two morals to this story:  
  a) Industry standard benchmarks are good to get an
     approximate feel for the performance of various machines, but the real
     test is to run your own codes in your own work environment (i.e. how
     many users will be running, how much compiling do you do, how much
     number crunching do you do, how much database work do you do?).

  b) Benchmarks are run by experienced technical people who work
     for the computer vendors.  The average user often does not have
     that much experience for squeezing the most out of their machines.

