From: Larry McVoy <lm@slovax.eng.sun.com>
Organization: Sun Microsystems, Inc.  Mt. View, Ca.

I'm a systems architect at Sun, designing Sun's future product line of
high performance, highly available servers.  I spend a great deal of time 
on benchmarks and I think they all pretty much stink.  The benchmarks
spend far too much time trying to measure high level operations.  The
problem with this is that problems at the core of the OS are magnified
but *not* identified by such benchmarks.

For example, suppose context switching is slow.  You go run some multi
user benchmark and it is slow.  Your engineers spend months tracking it
back to context switching.  Wouldn't it be far better to have benchmarks
that measured the core features of an OS such as:

	. system calls (latency: write one byte to /dev/null)
	. system calls (scaling: write one byte to /dev/null * N processes)
	. context switching (latency: two process benchmark)
	. context switching (scaling/throughput: N process benchmark)
	. signal processing (usually buried in the ctxswitch test)
	. process creation (measure fork & vfork, exec, and exit)
	. disks (throughput: sequential file system & raw device)
	. disks (latency: random file system & raw device)
	. disks (scaling: multiple arms all doing sequential, add one at a time)
	. networks (throughput: ftp test)
	. networks (latency: tcp transaction test, send a byte back and forth)
	. networks (scaling: multiple ftp/tcp_xact tests)

And on all of these report CPU cycles used per MB and/or per operation?
Better yet, normalize the CPU cycles on SPECint92 so that you can really
compare the software.  Ideally, you report both the raw numbers (lets
vendors show off their hot box/disk/whatever) as well as the normalized
numbers.

The point is to pick the set of features that get used, measure them at
the lowest possible level first, and only after those numbers are
reasonable, do you worry about high level benchmarks.  High level
benchmarks don't tend to help vendors or customers understand why the
system is slow/fast, and frequently, knowing why is the most
informative thing.

Let me know what you think about this.  If it is an interesting topic to
you and your magazine, perhaps a short article on benchmarking might be
appropriate?
--
