From: Pete Gallanis <pete@ct.covia.com>

Almost four years ago, Covia Partnership, the owner of the Apollo 
Computer Reservation System based in Denver, began an investigation 
into the viability of parallel processing as a peer in its mainframe 
computing environment.  Early on in the research we discovered that 
the initial direction the vendors of this technology were headed was 
towards scalable data bases for decision support, and they measured 
performance in TPC's or other conventional benchmarks.  Since these 
statistics did not match our high volume OLTP environment with 
peaks of over 2,000 transactions per second, we were forced to develop 
our own benchmarking facility.  The result of our efforts provided us 
with the ability to compare the throughput and scalability of a variety 
of platforms using models of our current and projected workloads.

In our case, we had no choice but to develop and use benchmarks.  The 
technology was too new (in most cases, even conventional benchmark 
statistics were unavailable), and our direction was beyond what most 
vendors had tested.

