In <1993Jul12.213229.27513@mustang.mst6.lanl.gov> carter@ferrari.mst6.lanl.gov (Dave Carter) writes:
>i'm trying to set up high speed (14.4K) modems on the serial ports on
>a DECstation 5000, running ultrix 4.2a.  they are USRobotics couriers.

here is a followup, in case you're interested.  (i finally succeeded.)
as it turns out, my problems could have been solved immediately if i
hadn't been using a brain-dead version of kermit!

1) i was able to lock in the serial rate at 19200, using &B1.  i had
originally assumed that &B1 locked in whatever rate the getty process
was set to, 8bit.19200 when the getty process was started.  this is
not quite right: the modem uses the baud rate which is written in
nvram, even if the tty line on the decstation is set at 19200.  i had
used kermit to change the modem settings, (storing them with an &W).
unfortunately, the kermit i was using only went to 9600, so this was
written into nvram.  i got a new version of kermit, connected to the
tty at 19200, and re-stored the settings into nvram.  wala.

2) the block errors appearing in the syslog, "getty: /dev/tty00:
Operation would block" were indeed being caused by always asserting
DSR, causing the brain-dead getty behavior, as someone kindly pointed
out here.  (this is the default on all of my various courier modems, i
noticed.)  this problem also caused annoying delays while trying to
connect.  however, when i tried to change the modem setting from &S0
to &S1, i was always disconnected from the tty line, and it would not
allow me to reconnect, giving an error "Operation would block".  (i
also got disconnected after changing various other settings.)  when i
got the new version of kermit, this no longer happened.  i can now
correctly change all the settings on the modems.  in fact, i no longer
have to turn off the ttys in /etc/ttys in order to kermit out to the
modems, as i had to with the old kermit.  also, it is not necessary to
override DTR.  connections are now made immediately, like they're
supposed to.

to summarize, the only settings i changed from the factory defaults
are &B1, &H1, &R2 and &S1.  switches 3 & 8 are down, all the rest up.
(these are on courier HST and V.32bis modems.)

						- dave
