>Technically there is no limit, although other volume constraits may prevent
>you from adding another entry to an exisiting directory.
>

<this is being written based on experiences several years ago - if I'm
 wrong, no flames, just corrections>

However, there is a practical limit - how long do you want to wait on
directory operations.  As the directory gets very large, certain operations,
most notoriously file deletions can take an exceeeeeeeedingly long time.

As an experiment, we tried to see how many 9.3 files you could stuff in
a directory (at the time, there was a 1024 block limit to a directory)
so I created a stupid shell^H^H^H^H^H DCL proc to create 0 length files
until VMS blew it out.  I don't remember the number created but it was
a respectable number.

The rub came when deleting this mess.  DEL/NOLOG *.*.*....  Delete would
remove each file from the directory in sequence and empty the first
directory block.  It (RMS/XQP ?) would recognize the empty block and
copy the remaining n-1 blocks down and truncate the directory.  This
went on for - I think - 11 hours.

Our recommendation to our customers was keep the size down.
