>  Bullshit.  A well-designed compiler isn't io-limited.

Thank you for your kind words.  Most of the UNIX compilers I've seen
and the HP and ALPHA are *NOT UNIQUE* in this, communicate between the
passes (cpp, possibly two compile passes, an optimization pass, the
assembler) with files in /tmp.  Linking, while not strictly compilation
but many people assume it is, is almost *ALL* I/O...it's primary function
is searching library symbols for matches to the undefined symbols already
present.  The relocation is very efficient in itself.

The problem with many of these fast machines is that they can compile faster
than they can read and write the disks.  They are I/O limitted....If you have
a doubt, try sticking /tmp on a substantially slower or faster disk and see
what that does to the elapsed time.

_Ron


