WRONG!  UNIX was designed for the ASR-37.  That beast has rectangular keys
with rounded corners.  If UNIX had trully been designed for the 33, why
would everything be in lower case?  There were hacks (LCASE mode anyone)
for people stuck with 33's and other braindead terminals.  The Model 37
is distinctive in that it has a large key on the keyboard that sends
a LF character labeled "NEW LINE."  Does this sound familiar to you?

The Model 37 plods along at 150 baud.  It does upper and lower case.  It
has the ability to have a greek type box installed that you switch to with
SI and SO control codes.  It also has a two color ribbon and is capable of
half line vertical positioning (i.e., it knows what to do with the ESC-8's
and ESC-9's that nroff puts out by default).  Another cute feature is that
the motor doesn't start up until DATASET READY is asserted on the RS-232
port and their is a green PROCEED light that comes on when CARRIER DETECT
is asserted.

Anybody want to buy one cheap?  I still have all the tech manuals for it
too?

-Ron


