sspatter@netcom.com (Spatters) writes:
>      How do I access above 64K on my PDP-11/34a from the octal console?
> I am trying to write a little mini-os for my machine but I can't figure
> out how to get beyond 177,777 Octal.  I'm fine with that being a code-

Do you mean the (optional) programmer's panel or the console emulator routine
that the machine cold-starts into?

The programmer's panel is 18-bit by nature, but the console emulator is a
real live pdp11 program, and as such is constrained by the 11's address 
space.  To get outside the 16-bit world, you have to reach in and diddle
the APRs yourself; the user's manual tell you how to do this.  Basically,
you do manually what real code has to do to get to the memory above 56k.

One word of warning -- if you unmap the I/O page, you can't reach the APRs
any more to re-map it.....

