> Computer languages MUST evolve towards the common language of the
> human race, which has very old origins, whether that language be French, 
> English, Russian, German or whatever.  The structure of natural language
> is in our genes.

This statement appears to be drawing unfair support against Unix.  You
cannot argue that "anti-Unix naming tendencies" are inherent in the
human genetic code.  Or that anything which does not employ natural
language is not human.

As human's develop, we notice a divergence between the highly
intelligent, and the "average" person.  Every human being has a certain
task that they want to accomplish, and they will go at it with their
best speed.  For extremely bright and inventive people, the current
standards of human communication (indeed, even English in some cases)
are simply too cumbersome to accommodate the speed of thought that goes
on in these people's minds.  Therefore, they look for shortcuts, and
ways to improve their usage of time.

Shorthand is example of this.  An excellent secretary is encumbered by
longhand script.  And so he will, if he has the skill, opt to use
shorthand instead of longhand.  Not because it's cryptic and makes him
different than others, but because it's the best environment for him to
work in.

Many "geniuses" develop their own writing styles, or their own
alphabets, just because they want to interact with _themselves_
efficiently.  If you'd been given a 110 baud teletype as a child, but
found it heinously slow to work on, you might have, if it'd bothered you
enough, even  gone to such lengths as to reinvent the teletype.  This is
not a strange idea; it has been done before.  "Necessity is the mother
of invention."

The same goes for Unix.  My claim is not that Unix people represent the
intelligentsia of our population (that _would_ be an ego trip).  What I
am saying is that in most cases Unix programmers have developed their
skills to a degree where "intuitiveness" and "plain language" are just
mind-numbingly cumbersome.  Who wants to type "copy" when you can type
"cp"?  Once you've gotten past the one day learning curve, dropping two
letters from a command is no problem.  And if you type that command a
thousand times each month, that's a keystroke savings of 2000.  Multiply
this across all the most commonly used commands, and it adds up to a
substantial gain in efficiency.

The moral is: if something is dead weight for you, drop it.  It doesn't
matter if other people are going to have problems with it, because we're
talking about how YOU interact with YOURSELF.  I'm not about to type
"compile program john.text" instead of "cc j.c" just because somebody
else in the world doesn't understand it.

This does not, however, invalidate the need for more intuitive and
intelligible operating system interfaces.  Unix does deserve blame for
it blatant lack of concern for the general user community.  But what we
need is not a "flat leveling" of the OS design!  We need _grades_ of
user interface complexity.  Something that could be tailored to each
user's tastes.  Many program today have an "expert" mode, which is meant
just for experts.  If Unix had had something like a "beginner mode", it
would have attracted more attention in the public eye that it has so
far.

So while I agree that a simpler interface design is a must if Unix is
going to become accessible to everyone, I cannot accept the statement
that terse interface design goes against human nature, or that it
doesn't have its place, or that it is only used by people who want to
assert their egos over the rest of the world.

Thanks for listening,
  John
