   In my Humble Opinion,  About the origins of unix:

   MOUNT SOAPBOX/AGAIN

   Naw, it was just a smart-assed manifestation of rebellion, a way of
  creating an in-crowd of young-person tech weenies, an new culture.  It was
  rebellion against the establishment, change the world idea so common to
  young people and those who want to think "young", and think they know it
  all.  Some are political, some are technical, some are musical, some are
  artistic, some are crazy, and some are more frowned on by society.  We
  were all there once.   A case of re-inventing the wheel.  It happens
  strong every 20 years after a major world disaster.  Sometimes they come
  up with something good.  The 1957 Chev., especially for only 12 years
  after WWII was great, as was some of the Beatles' music and the Apple
  computer.  What's your favourite invention of the last 50 years?  I
  think the 1948 invention of the transistor has got to be the start.

    I remember when I got out of school, being an audio fanatic and a
  musician, I wanted to rid the world of carbon microphones (you know, 
  the kind invented by Alexander Graham Bell the Canadian inventor of
  the telephone).  Since then, I have grown to respect the robustness
  of the carbon microphone (still used in telephones today) in tough
  environments (like heat, cold, large magnetic fields, large electric
  fields, large radiation fields, bad vibration, bad atmosphere, high
  humidity, low humidity, high voltage, power surges, immersion in
  strange liquids (spit, beer, bathroom water), foul language and people
  with bad breath).  And, if it acts up a little, just give it a bang
  on the table and it's as good as new.  Works almost every time.  Carbon
  is so robust.  So I didn't invent my new microphone.  But the tech
  weenies did invent unix and now society has to pay the piper for
  something that by its very nature is both overspecialized and
  oversimplified at the same time.  Sure it runs fast, but your car
  engine runs faster without a transmission and a drive train, too.

