|>
|>What is the maximum number of files (not versions) allowed under VMS in a
|>directory on a Files-11 disk?
|>

Technically there is no limit, although other volume constraits may prevent
you from adding another entry to an exisiting directory.

If the current directory file is full, a new contiguous one must be allocated.
This new directory file must be equal to the current size, plus room for
new records. If there is not enough contigous disk space available, or
the volume's file header is full or you have exceeded your disk quota the
file creation operation will not succeed.

Other reasons are related to directory file corruption, caused by bad media,
inconsistant directories cause by system or disk failures while a directory
update was in progress, even some disk-defraggers have been known to cause
problems.

An ANALYZE/DISK will show any directory inconsistencies, adding /REPAIR should
fix them.

