|> though, maybe that makes a difference.  Making the serial line lock at
|> 19200 is a modem setup issue, not a getty issue.  The "would block"
|> messages in syslog are still there. Also, the brain dead getty raises and
|> lowers DTR while waiting for an incoming call, and the modem won't
|> answer when DTR is down, so you'll need DTR overide in the modem too.

I use a DS5000/200 here at 38400. I have also used it at 19200.
I use both SLIP and uucp, both incoming and outgoing, using
hardware flow control to ZyXEL 1496 E+ modems. This all works
quite fine.

The trick to getting 38400 is to flip a bit before _every_ boot.
Then pretend to use 19200, which will result in 38400 being
selected. To flip the bit of interrest, perform the following:

	>> e 0xbff00000
	[[ some value is printed ]]
	[[ in your head, OR in the value 0x100 ]]
	>> d 0xbff00000 {value|0x100}
	>> boot

I *believe* that all recent versions of Ultrix do NOT stomp on
this bit, which when true tells the UARTS to run at 38400 and
when 0 tells the UARTS to run at 19200, when the 19200 baud rate
is selected via the tty driver (stty or equiv). Your milage may
vary, and you may have to go mung a piece of the kernel to get
this to work, if your driver bashes the bit described.

