Organization: Drexel University, College of Engineering, Philadelphia, PA
Lines: 39


  I've just finished setting up a bunch of modems on some 5000/240's,
also DECserver 700's, which also seem to have the identical RS232 pinouts
and operation.  What I've found:

1. Get a full RS232 cables (or at least pins 2-8 + 20, straight through
2. For the modem, have it 
	a. assert DSR only when carrier detect (&S1)
	b. CD follows real carrier carrier detect (&C1)
	c. reset modem when DTR drops (&D2)
	d. use RTS/CTS flow control (&K3)
	e. turn off echoing (E0)

3. The serial ports of the 5000 (at least 240, don't know about other models)
   can do 38400 baud.  Duplicate a /etc/gettytab entry for 19200 and replace
   with 38400.  Also change the /etc/ttys entry.  Tip can't talk at 38400
   but uucp can.  
4. When RTS/CTS is connected HW flow control will work, nothing special
   required (I've gotten the DECserver ports set at 57600 with f/c and they
   work fine.
5. Set you modem so it autobauds the modem-modem connection but the serial
   port speed remains locked.  A Codex modem I worked on had front panel
   controls that allowed setting of the speed.  With Zoom modems (V32.bis
   specifically), the port settings (speed, parity, databits) at time of
   last AT&W are what the port remain locked at.
6. I've set the port to shared and created a uucp L.sys entry (hostname
   reset) that connects to the port directory, issues and AT&F (reset to
   factory defaults), all the required AT settings, and then an AT&W.  We
   use it to reset the modems, also all we have to do is connect a new 
   modem, say 'uupoll reset', and it properly configures it.

  With this setup I've been able to have dialins from V32.bis (with zmodem
file transfers of over 3100cps), to lowly 1200 connections, with no loss
of characters.  

   Hope this helps.

					Jeff White
					jeff@cbis.ece.drexel.edu


