|>Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec
|>
|>At the moment I am trying put in a delay into the event loop
|>of some program to slow it down.
|>
|>I'm attempting to use the select() function to
|>select an invalid socket and using the timeout
|>for the delay. For example,
|>
|>struct timeval delay;
|>
|>delay.tv_sec = 0;
|>delay.tv_usec = 200000;
|>
|>select(0,0,0,0,&delay);
|>
|>this snippet should delay the processor for 1/5 th of
|>a second.
|>
|>BUT, it doesn't seems to work. Why????
|>
|>Some background info:
|>
|>it works on a HP.
|>Oddly, select() should be in time.h but
|>is in fact in socket.h despite what the help says.
|>
|>I've compiled the code on an Alpha using the /stand=vaxc
|>option.
|>
|>Any help would be much appreciated.
|>
|>Peter
|>
|>

DEC C on VMS does not implement select() for general file descriptors.  There is
a special version of select that only works with socket descriptors.

I put this on the wishlist.  In the meantime,  you could a VMS specific routine
(such as lib$wait ) to wait for a small amount of time.    [If you're interested
lib$wait is documented under $ HELP RTL LIB LIB$WAIT.  It takes an float by
reference]

