In article <GSHAPIRO.93Jul7092612@monkeyboy.WPI.EDU>, gshapiro@monkeyboy.WPI.EDU (Gregory Neil Shapiro) writes:
>
>If you plan on upgrading any machines to DEC's new R4000/R4400 MIPS
>CPU, be aware that something in the root partition of older ULTRIX
>versions won't work with the new CPUs (we suppose it is probably the
>bootstrap).
>
>The symtoms are that after installing the new board are trying to
>boot, the disk will spin up, read the boot blocks and then the station
>will reinitialize and try again.  No messages are output.

What's not (really) clear from this description is what version of ULTRIX
you're booting when it fails.  Kernels before V4.3A have no knowledge
of the CPU type for the R4000 upgrades.  When an earlier kernel searches
it's table of known and supported CPUs it won't find the new ones and
should panic.  Older versions of ULTRIX would sometimes just hang.

It's concieveable that V4.3A included new boot blocks that the
installation and upgrade procedures knew how to install.  The
write back cache of the R4000 is different enough that older
boot code may not work, so a replacement was needed.
 

--
Alan Rollow				alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com



