>the '87.  Awesome.  Got to be careful with these cartridges now...

That's TZ85s and TZ86s.

For TK50s and 70s, as soon as a drive sees a raw tape, it writes a calibration
track. If you bulk erase the tape, you can make a Compact Tape (tk50) work
where a Compact II tape goes (tk70) but as was pointed out, you are risking
things. The Compact media is not qualified to Compact II standards. Kinda like
using single density floppies in double density drives.

For TK85s and 86s, the routine is a little different. In the case of the 86,
the format can be changed when a write-from-bot is performed. In other
words, a TK86 tape can be converted to a TK85 and vice versa by the drive.

With TK85 drives, it's a moot point since it only writes 85 format. Do not,
under any circumstances, attempt to write 85 format on tk50/70 media. (Come
to think of it, I've never tried it and am not sure it's even possible...)

Now with the 87s, ....ummmm, I'll get back to you on that. :-)

Mickey. DEC tape engineering.


