In article <1993Jul2.005915.5097@bvc.edu>, tim@bvc.edu writes:
>
>Hi,
>
>    On a MicroVax 3100-90, what is the recommended maximum number of SCSI
>drives that I can attach to the machine before I take a performance hit?  Any
>drives that I attach will have a very high I/O rate.

From a bandwidth viewpoint, 2 RZ26s can pretty easily saturate the
typical 5 MB/sec SCSI.  For most I/O loads the available bandwidth
will be come the limiting factor before the number I/Os and I doubt
that will much of a problem unless you have Command Queueing.

So, for your high I/O rate, take the average I/O size, multiply times
the number per second and see how MB you're trying to move per second.
If it's much less than 5 MB/sec you probably don't have to worry.  If
you're getting close to 5 MB/sec, then it gets hard to guess.

>
>    Or, on a related note:  What MicroVax configuration would you recommend to
>replace a VAX6320 with 4 RA92's on an HSC, that would perform as well or
>better?  I am not yet able to move to an Alpha system. (political reasons)

Are you CPU bound or I/O bound?  Nearly any of the faster VAX 4000 systems
(not MicroVAX) will be faster than the VAX 6320.  The HSC was probably 
way overkill for only four disks, but it offers some performance advantages
in some I/O loads.  You'd probably want to replace the RA92s with DSSI
or SCSI disks with similar capacity.  DSSI might have similar performance
in the circumstances where the HSC did well, but SCSI is likely to be a
better growth path.


