In article <211dm4$bei@nic.umass.edu>, kimmel@nic.umass.edu (Matt Kimmel,A155 LGRC,(413) 545-1607,) writes:
...
|>                                               So my question is, is there any
|> way from VMS, Standalone Backup, or Ultrix that I can "reformat" these tapes
|> to an "unused" state, so I can write new stuff to them from my TK70?

The ONLY way to do this is to degauss the tapes in a bulk eraser of some
sort, so as to erase the calibration marks which the drive writes to a virgin
tape when it is first loaded. There's nothing you can do from the machine which
will do it.

Make sure the cartridges really are inscribed "CompacTape II".  The older tapes
(plain "CompacTape") MAY APPEAR to work at high density in a TK70, but they are
not specified to work and you stand a greater than usual chance of losing your
data.
 
|> And while we're on the subject, what exactly is the difference between the
|> TK50 and TK70 drives?

The TK70 is just a re-engineered version of the same basic idea - it works
better, faster and gets more onto a tape. I believe the last is achieved using a
combination of higher linear density and a greater number of tracks across the
