***************************************************
** Disclaimer:                                   **
**                                               **
** Take this as a personal opinion, and not as a **
** official DIGITAL statement or position... nor **
** is it a committment by DIGITAL for anything.  **
***************************************************


I'm currently working on VMS X11 graphics for this machine,
and since a lot of the code is comming from the OSF group,
I can assure you that it is also being worked on by OSF ;-)
and I can also assure you, that after a late start, we are
working to put X11/Motif support on the system as soon as 
possible.  No, I can't give you dates.  But at least I can
tell you that it *is* being actively worked on.

I don't think that this is due to a cynical ploy to force
Unix and VMS users to buy the DEC 3000 (which I also worked
on, and which is quite a piece of hardware for a fairly
small price tag - the 3000/300L is *very* inexpensive).

Instead I think this was the result of an evolution of
thought inside DEC about just what a "server" is versus a
workstation.  The traditional view in DEC is that a server
doesn't have graphics.  Sometimes graphics can be added (like
the QDSS on a VAX 4000) but it is not part of the design or
requirements.  Workstations on the other hand are characterized
by a high level of graphics performance and functionality.

This new system falls someplace in between these two.  The
current HX option on the TURBOchannel runs rings around the
Triton on the EISA, and the next generation of HX cards will
be *amazingly* fast.  So, it's not particularly interesting as
a "workstation".  So, since it's not a workstation, it must
be a "server" -- which doesn't need graphics!

And those who view the system as a PC server, naturally think
of the system in terms of Windows NT.

However, I think there is now a realization that the competition
for this class of machine includes base graphics - regardless
of the operating system.  And that SVGA/EISA graphics may be
"good enough" for a great many people (and in fact, I've been
running Windows NT on it and am quite happy with the graphics
performance -- as a software developer).  But that it's lack
could discourage some people from buying the machine.

