In article <C9EAuq.pt@pfm.rmt.sub.org>, rcr@pfm.rmt.sub.org
(Rainer C. Ruppert) writes:

rr>Hi Netters,
rr>does anybody outthere know if dec is having a unix-based operating
rr>system with enhanced security features ?

    [I am assuming that you are not refering to Berkeley `enhanced'
    security, but rather are refering to NCSC or ITSEC security.]

    ULTRIX MLS+ is under National Computer Security Center (NCSC)
    evaluation for TCSEC Class B1 security.  In addition to the
    Class B1 features, ULTRIX MLS+ adds the feaures of a DIA 
    Compartmented mode workstation (CMW).

And Dan Wing (dwing@uh01.colorado.edu) replies:

dw>Ultrix is supposed to have some enhanced security that can be optionally
dw>enabled.

dw>But DEC is abandoning Ultrix.

    I personally think the word `abandoning' is a little strong, but this
    is obviously a matter of opinion.  I would check with the sites that
    are running DEC OSF/1 before deciding on the use of `abandoned'.

    As for security -- Digital has committed to porting all ULTRIX MLS+
    security features to the DEC OSF/1 kernel on the Alpha AXP platform.
    This obviously includes adding Class B1 and CMW security features to
    the Open Software Foundation's OSF/1 base -- such as multi-level
    X Windows extensions and multi-level network security.

dw> I hate to ask this, for fear of serious flames from the Unix folks, but
dw> what about VMS, or SE-VMS?  VMS V6.0 is rated C2, and SE-VMS has a B
dw> rating, I believe.

    A humble correction on current status: OpenVMS is in (the late stages
    of) final evaluation for Class C2 and the Security Enhanced OpenVMS
    (SEVMS) is a similar stage of final evaluation for Class B1 security.
    There is work under way to allow SEVMS and ULTRIX MLS+ to interoperate
    in a secure multi-level fashion, as well.

dw>Some vendors have C2-rated Unix systems, too.

    There is a list of all evaluated and currently-under-evaluation
    products available.  (I don't happen to have a current copy of
    this list -- the list is located on dockmaster if nowhere else.)

dw>However, the security of your OS doesn't do diddly for the security of your
dw>database application -- what you really need is a database that allows for
dw>record, if not element-level, security which is enforced by the database.

    SERdb, a secure multi-level database, operates on SEVMS.  (Not to
    slight any other multi-level database systems available...)   Trusted
    Oracle and Trusted Informix are available for ULTRIX MLS+.  (And again,
    not to slight...)

    For more information on the avalability and/or current evalutation
    status of these or other Digital security products, or for futures,
    please contact your local Digital sales office.

