ron@topaz.bds.com (Ron Natalie) writes:
[wow. a first. I catch myself publicly trying to rebut Ron. ;) ]

>There are C2 and B rated UNIX systems out there.  However, they are a royal
>pain in the ass, and don't do much for real security concerns.  It's just
>to keep all the orange-book lawyers happy.

	I have to disagree with this.

	There's something to be said for the ideas behind orange
book evaluation and all the pain it entails. It's got to do with
trust and process. For example, we all know that the security
clearance process isn't perfect. Some bad apples slip through.
BUT it helps to have a set of procedures in place that raise the
degree to which you can rely on the system (i.e.: trust). For
example, lots of UNIX vendors sell machines with "C2 functionality."
That means exactly squat. It means "we took a stab at this but
we didn't even try to check to see if we got it right."  Putting
the stuff through evaluation means you actually tried to see if
you got it right. Having the "C2 functionality" at least means
that if you choose to enable it, you can assume the system has
the required types of logging and basic security stuff.

	Going through evaluation, depending on the level, has
some really neat requirements that I, as someone who has often
been nauseated by low-quality code from vendors, really like.
Some of the evaluation levels make the simple step of requiring
that bugs identified in the security of the system must be fixed.

	That's not useless.

	I agree that Orange Book taken in a vacuum doesn't give
you much. But it provides a framework for talking about the
kinds of problems you've got to deal with. Evaluation shows
that the folks who did the system actually tried to come
to grips with those problems. That's cool. From a pragmatic
point of view, I think a lot of people find the Orange Book
to be idealistic. They want to run X-windows at high security
levels. They give up completely and just lock the building and
run everything at TS - whatever. The evaluation process is
expensive and very very slow. The worst part is that it acts
as a ball-and-chain on software evolution, since you can't
change your TCB without following procedures.

	But I find it useful that others have thought about
these problems and tried to formalize a methodology for
addressing them. Otherwise I'd be left with just my own
little brain.

mjr.


